  🐇❗️ 🤐 data 📇 ➡️ 📇 🍇
    📏 data❓ ➡️ size
    💭 The fixed codes of RFC 1951, 3.2.6, turned round once so that writing one is
    💭 two readings. 0 to 143 have eight bits, 144 to 255 nine, 256 to 279 seven and the
    💭 rest eight.
    🆕🍨🐚🔢🍆 0 288❗️ ➡️ 🖍🆕codes
    🆕🍨🐚🔢🍆 8 288❗️ ➡️ 🖍🆕widths
    🔂 symbol 🆕⏩ 0 288❗️ 🍇
      ↪️ symbol ◀️ 144 🍇
        🔄🐇🎈 0x30 ➕ symbol 8❗️ ➡️ 🐽codes symbol❗️
      🍉
      🙅↪️ symbol ◀️ 256 🍇
        🔄🐇🎈 0x190 ➕ symbol ➖ 144 9❗️ ➡️ 🐽codes symbol❗️
        9 ➡️ 🐽widths symbol❗️
      🍉
      🙅↪️ symbol ◀️ 280 🍇
        🔄🐇🎈 symbol ➖ 256 7❗️ ➡️ 🐽codes symbol❗️
        7 ➡️ 🐽widths symbol❗️
      🍉
      🙅↪️ 👍 🍇
        🔄🐇🎈 0xC0 ➕ symbol ➖ 280 8❗️ ➡️ 🐽codes symbol❗️
      🍉
    🍉

    🆕💦❗️ ➡️ bits
    💭 The ten bytes every gzip stream begins with: what it is, that deflate follows, no
    💭 name, no time, and "written on a system nobody need know".
    🔂 lead 🍿 0x1F 0x8B 8 0 0 0 0 0 0 0xFF 🍆 🍇
      💧 bits lead 8❗️
    🍉
    💭 One block, the last, with the fixed codes.
    💧 bits 1 1❗️
    💧 bits 1 2❗️
    🔂 i 🆕⏩ 0 size❗️ 🍇
      💧🐇🧮 data i❗️ ➡️ byte
      💧 bits 🐽 codes byte❗️ 🐽 widths byte❗️❗️
    🍉
    💭 256 is "the block ends here".
    💧 bits 🐽 codes 256❗️ 🐽 widths 256❗️❗️
    🏁 bits❗️
